partialText:
&lt;p&gt;Email verification looks like a small form until you ship it. Then the edge cases arrive: the request is still running, the code is wrong, the link expired, the user clicked resend twice, or the browser came back from a suspended tab.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The UI problem is usually an unclear state model
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Many React forms start with three booleans:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;loading&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;setLoading&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;setError&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;verified&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;setVerified&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;This is friendly at first, but it can describe impossible combinations. What does &lt;code&gt;loading: true&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;verified: true&lt;/code&gt; mean? Should an old error remain visible while a new request is running? Can the resend button be enabled during a code submission?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The important part are the transitions, not the number of variables. A verification flow should tell the user what is happening and what action is safe next.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Model verification as a small state machine
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Start with named states that match product behavior. A TypeScript discriminated union makes the rules visible in the component:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;VerificationState&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"idle"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"sending"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"awaiting-code"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;expiresAt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;resendAvailableAt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;number&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"verifying"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;code&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"verified"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"error"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;message&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;canRetry&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;boolean&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"expired"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Now the UI has a smaller decision surface. An &lt;code&gt;awaiting-code&lt;/code&gt; state must carry the timing information needed by the countdown. A &lt;code&gt;verified&lt;/code&gt; state does not need an error message. The compiler helps keep these informations together when the flow changes later.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I like this approach because it connects product language to implementation language. “Check your inbox” becomes &lt;code&gt;awaiting-code&lt;/code&gt;, while “Your code expired” becomes &lt;code&gt;expired&lt;/code&gt;. This makes a confusing email flow a little more clear for both the user and the next developer.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Render states with a discriminated union
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Keep rendering close to the state model, but keep side effects in event handlers or a dedicated hook:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;VerificationPanel&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;VerificationState&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;switch&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;case&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"idle"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"button"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Send&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;verification&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;email&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;case&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"sending"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;role&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"status"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Sending&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;your&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;verification&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;email&lt;/span&gt;…&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;case&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"awaiting-code"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;CodeForm&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;expiresAt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;expiresAt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;case&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"verifying"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;role&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"status"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Checking&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;the&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;code&lt;/span&gt;…&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;case&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"verified"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;role&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"status"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Email&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;verified&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;You&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;can&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;continue&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;case&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"expired"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"button"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Send&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;code&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;case&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"error"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;role&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"alert"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
          &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;message&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
          &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;canRetry&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"button"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Try&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;again&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The UI should not guess from a partially updated collection of flags. It should announce the current state, disable actions that cannot succeed, and preserve focus when an error replaces the form. A status message that appears only visually is not enough; screen readers need a useful announcement too.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Keep resend and expiration explicit
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Resend is where many otherwise clean implementations become flaky. Treat it as a separate transition with a server-side policy behind it. The client countdown is useful feedback, but it is not the source of truth.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When the user submits a code, capture the request identity. If an older request finishes after a newer one, ignore the stale result. A simple sequence number works for a small hook:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;requestId&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useRef&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;verify&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;code&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;currentId&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;++&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;requestId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"verifying"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;code&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;verifyCode&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;code&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;currentId&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;requestId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ok&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="o"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"verified"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"error"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;message&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"That code is not valid."&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;canRetry&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;This small guard prevents a slow response from moving a fresh form backwards. It also make the failure behavior much easier to explain in a test.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For temporary test inboxes, a service such as &lt;a href="https://tempmailso.com" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;tempmailso&lt;/a&gt; can help isolate verification data during development. Do not use an inbox you do not control for real customer accounts, and do not treat a successful test message as proof that production delivery is healthy.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Test the transitions, not just the happy path
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Component tests should cover state changes and user-visible consequences:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Sending starts with one click and announces progress.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A successful response renders the code form.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;An invalid code keeps the user in a recoverable error state.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A stale response cannot overwrite a newer request.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Resend is unavailable during its cooldown and becomes available afterward.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Expiration offers a clear next action.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is also where &lt;a href="https://dev.to/jasonmills94/tie-eks-alerts-to-one-rollout-4kei"&gt;connecting one alert to one rollout&lt;/a&gt; is a useful operational idea: make each failure point explainable instead of collapsing several causes into “Something went wrong.” For account flows, &lt;a href="https://dev.to/sophiax99/safer-email-change-flows-start-with-proof-11ea"&gt;proof-first email change flows&lt;/a&gt; offer the same mindset from a security angle.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;During manual testing, try a tem email, paste a code twice, navigate away while the request is pending, and return after the expiry time. These cases finds the gaps that a single successful test will miss. They are boring checks, but boring checks is exactly what protects a sign-up flow from surprise.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  A short shipping checklist
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Before shipping, check that:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Every visible message maps to one explicit state.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Submit, resend, and continue actions have clear disabled rules.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Stale responses cannot overwrite current state.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Expiration and retry behavior are tested.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Status and error messages work with keyboard and screen readers.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The server still enforces verification expiry and resend limits.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An explicit state machine does not make email delivery perfect. It does make the product honest about what it knows, what it is waiting for, and what the user can do next. That clarity is a feature worth shipping.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A user opens a page, sees a button, and clicks it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Your automated test opens the same page, sees the same button, and fails.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Sometimes the button is replaced a moment later. Sometimes a loading skeleton looks enough like the real component to confuse a selector. Sometimes the browser's address bar changes the available space, and an element moves just as the test tries to click it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The application might be broken. The test might be broken. Or both might be behaving exactly as designed, just at different moments.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's what makes modern browser testing frustrating. We build increasingly dynamic interfaces, then write tests as if pages appear all at once and stay put.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  A visible page isn't necessarily an interactive page
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Consider an app using React Suspense and server-side rendering.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The server sends HTML. The browser displays it. A person looking at the screen thinks the page is ready. But JavaScript may still be loading, data may still be resolving, and React may still be attaching event handlers or replacing parts of the DOM.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Your test finds the Save button and clicks it. Nothing happens. So someone adds a two-second wait.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That seems to fix it, until CI gets a little slower.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The better approach is to test readiness, not elapsed time. Wait for the specific control to become usable or for the application to expose the state that means the interaction can succeed. If a component passes through loading, hydrated, and interactive states, make those states observable.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This &lt;a href="https://vibiumlabs.com/how-to-make-browser-tests-survive-react-suspense-streaming-ssr-and-partial-hydration/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;guide to testing React Suspense, streaming SSR, and partial hydration&lt;/a&gt; lays out the important distinction between slow data, replaced DOM nodes, and lost focus. Those failures can look identical from the outside, but they need different fixes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A longer sleep is not a diagnosis.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Your selector may be perfectly valid and still be wrong
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Now imagine a dashboard with a list of customer accounts.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The test clicks the third row. It worked yesterday. Today, a new account arrives and the list reorders automatically. The third row is now a different customer.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The selector didn't fail. That's worse. It worked on the wrong thing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Skeleton loaders create another version of this problem. A test sees the container, assumes the content is ready, and attempts to interact with something that hasn't been rendered yet. Virtualized lists make it even trickier because rows may not exist in the DOM until they're scrolled into view.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A useful &lt;a href="https://bugbench.com/benchmark-plan-comparing-browser-automation-frameworks-on-dynamic-list-reordering-skeleton-loaders-and-selector-resilience/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;benchmark plan for dynamic lists, skeleton loaders, and selector resilience&lt;/a&gt; suggests measuring more than pass rate. It also looks at how often selectors need rewriting, how many special waits are required, and whether failures explain what actually happened.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That seems like a much better way to compare testing frameworks than clicking the same static login form 1,000 times.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For application tests, I would rather target an account by its stable identifier or accessible name than by its position on the page. And I would rather wait for a meaningful loaded state than assume the disappearance of one spinner proves everything is ready.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Checkout isn't one page. It's a series of handoffs.
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Payment flows expose the limits of simplistic browser tests particularly well.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A checkout form might live inside a third-party iframe. Then the provider redirects the user to a 3D Secure challenge. The browser returns to your site, perhaps after a reload, and the application has to recover the original order.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A test that only enters card details and looks for a success message can miss the worst failures.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Was the original payment intent preserved? Did the order remain associated with the correct customer? What happens if someone refreshes during the challenge or submits twice? Could you create a duplicate charge or order?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The &lt;a href="https://automated-testing-services.com/how-to-test-payment-iframe-flows-without-losing-state-across-reloads-redirects-and-3ds-challenges/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;guide to testing payment iframes, redirects, and 3DS flows&lt;/a&gt; approaches checkout as a state-preservation problem, not just a sequence of clicks.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's the right mental model. Use a payment provider's sandbox or test environment, exercise the redirect and challenge paths, and verify the backend result. The browser reaching a thank-you page is useful evidence, but it isn't proof that the payment was processed exactly once.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  On mobile, even the viewport is moving
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Desktop tests have a fairly stable idea of how much screen space is available.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Mobile browsers are different. The address bar expands and collapses. The on-screen keyboard appears. Safe-area insets protect content around notches and system UI. A fixed footer that looked fine in one screenshot can cover the only button a customer needs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is where pixel-perfect assertions can become counterproductive.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A &lt;a href="https://frontendtester.com/how-to-test-viewport-units-safe-area-insets-and-mobile-browser-chrome-changes-without-flaky-layout-assertions/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;practical guide to viewport units and safe-area testing&lt;/a&gt; makes a useful point: test whether content remains reachable and the layout responds correctly, not whether every coordinate remains identical after the browser chrome changes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For a mobile checkout, I'd want to know whether the Pay button stays visible or scrollable into view, whether the keyboard obscures an input, and whether fixed elements overlap important content.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Those are customer problems. A three-pixel difference in an unrelated margin usually isn't.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Visual tests need a review process, not just screenshots
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Visual regression testing sounds simple: take a screenshot, compare it with the baseline, and flag differences.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Then your team updates a shared button component and gets dozens of screenshot changes. Some are intentional. Some are real regressions. Someone has to tell them apart.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The more noisy the system gets, the easier it is to approve a change without looking carefully.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A &lt;a href="https://testautomationreviews.com/best-visual-testing-tools-for-design-system-teams-that-need-low-noise-and-clear-review-workflows/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;comparison of visual testing tools for design system teams&lt;/a&gt; evaluates the parts that matter after a difference is detected: noise control, baseline management, pull-request context, approvals, and ongoing maintenance.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's the interesting buying decision. Not just whether a tool can find differences, but whether people can review those differences quickly enough to keep trusting it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Test the transitions, not just the destinations
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If I were evaluating a browser testing setup today, I'd make a small collection of deliberately annoying scenarios before writing a large suite.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A page that hydrates after the HTML appears. A list that reorders during interaction. A payment flow that leaves the site and returns. A mobile footer that has to survive changing viewport height. A component update that produces a lot of legitimate visual differences.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Then I'd ask a few plain questions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Can the tests complete without arbitrary sleeps? When something fails, can we tell whether the app or the test is wrong? How much work does it take to maintain the selectors and review the screenshots?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Passing the happy path matters. But real applications spend a lot of time between states: loading, updating, redirecting, returning, resizing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's where customers find bugs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It's also where your tests need to earn their keep.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Imagine this scenario:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A customer clicks &lt;strong&gt;Place Order&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The request reaches your server, the database successfully creates the order, but the response never reaches the browser because of a network issue.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The customer assumes something went wrong and clicks again.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Now you have &lt;strong&gt;two orders instead of one&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is a common reliability problem in web applications.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Disabling a submit button might prevent accidental double-clicks, but it doesn't guarantee that an operation will execute only once.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Let's explore how to handle this properly using React, Node.js, and PostgreSQL.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  1. Frontend protection is only the first layer
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In React, we often prevent repeated submissions using a loading state:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;isSubmitting&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;setIsSubmitting&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;handleSubmit&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;isSubmitting&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setIsSubmitting&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="k"&gt;try&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;createOrder&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;finally&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setIsSubmitting&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;This is useful for improving the user experience.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But there is a problem.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Duplicate requests can still happen because of:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Network retries&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Multiple browser tabs&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Concurrent API calls&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Repeated webhook deliveries&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Client or server failures&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Even rapid calls within the same render cycle can observe the same React state before an update takes effect.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The important lesson: frontend protection improves UX, but backend protection ensures data integrity.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  2. Understanding idempotency
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An operation is idempotent when performing it multiple times has the same intended effect as performing it once.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;POST /api/orders
Idempotency-Key: order-abc-123
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;If the same request is submitted again with the same idempotency key, the server should recognize that the operation has already been processed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Instead of creating a new order, it can return the result of the original operation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is particularly important for:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Order creation&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Payment processing&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Subscription activation&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Inventory adjustments&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Financial transactions&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  3. Implementing idempotency in React
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We can generate a unique idempotency key using the browser's built-in Web Crypto API.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;useRef&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"react"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;OrderForm&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;idempotencyKeyRef&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useRef&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;createOrder&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;order&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Generate once for this intended operation.&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;idempotencyKeyRef&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="n"&gt;idempotencyKeyRef&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;crypto&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;randomUUID&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

    &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;fetch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"/api/orders"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="n"&gt;method&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"POST"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="n"&gt;headers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Content-Type"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"application/json"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Idempotency-Key"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;idempotencyKeyRef&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="n"&gt;body&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;JSON&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;stringify&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;order&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

    &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ok&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="k"&gt;throw&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Order request failed"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

    &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Use createOrder in your submission flow.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The key detail is that &lt;strong&gt;the same idempotency key must be reused when retrying the same intended operation&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Generate a new key only when the user starts a genuinely new order.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In a real application, you may need to persist the key beyond the component lifecycle if an unfinished operation must survive a page refresh.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  4. Protecting the database against duplicate inserts
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Frontend protection and idempotency middleware are useful, but concurrent requests can still introduce race conditions if the database isn't protected.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Consider this backend logic:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;existingOrder&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;findOrder&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;existingOrder&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;createOrder&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Looks fine, right?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Not necessarily.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Two requests can execute simultaneously:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;Request A                  Request B
    |                          |
Check existing order       Check existing order
    |                          |
Not found                  Not found
    |                          |
Create order               Create order
    |                          |
Success                    Success
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Both requests can pass the check before either creates the record.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is a classic check-then-insert race condition.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  A better approach: Database uniqueness constraints
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In PostgreSQL, enforce uniqueness at the database level.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight sql"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;CREATE&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;UNIQUE&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;INDEX&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;unique_order_request&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;ON&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;orders&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;user_id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;idempotency_key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;This assumes your orders table contains the corresponding columns.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Now use PostgreSQL's &lt;code&gt;ON CONFLICT&lt;/code&gt; functionality:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight sql"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;INSERT&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;INTO&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;orders&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;user_id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;idempotency_key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;product_id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;quantity&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;request_hash&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;VALUES&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt;$1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nv"&gt;$2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nv"&gt;$3&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nv"&gt;$4&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nv"&gt;$5&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;ON&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;CONFLICT&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;user_id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;idempotency_key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;DO&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;NOTHING&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;RETURNING&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;If the insert succeeds, the query returns the new order ID.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If another request already used the same key, PostgreSQL prevents a duplicate insert.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The backend can then retrieve the existing order.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One additional safeguard is important: compare the stored request hash with the incoming request's hash.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If the same idempotency key is reused with different order parameters, return a conflict response instead of silently accepting the changed request.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Database constraints provide protection that application-level checks alone cannot guarantee.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  5. What about transactions?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Preventing duplicate orders is only part of the problem.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Creating an order might involve multiple operations:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Create the order.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Reserve inventory.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Update related database records.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Record the transaction.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Send a notification.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If one operation fails halfway through, the system can end up in an inconsistent state.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Database transactions help ensure that related database modifications succeed or fail together.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;However, external operations such as sending emails or publishing messages are not automatically rolled back with a database transaction.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For these cases, patterns such as the &lt;strong&gt;transactional outbox&lt;/strong&gt; and idempotent event consumers can improve reliability.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is an important distinction when designing systems that interact with external services.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  6. Test real failure scenarios
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A feature working during normal testing doesn't necessarily mean it is reliable under failure conditions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here are five scenarios worth testing:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Scenario&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Expected behavior&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Two concurrent requests with the same key&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Only one order is created&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Retry after the original request commits&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Existing order is returned&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Same key with different request data&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Request is rejected&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Failed database transaction&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;No partial order is committed&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Two legitimate operations with different keys&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Both can proceed&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;These tests provide more confidence than simply checking whether a submit button becomes disabled.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  7. The approach I recommend
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For reliable applications, multiple layers should work together:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Frontend safeguards&lt;/strong&gt; improve the user experience and discourage accidental repeated submissions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Backend idempotency&lt;/strong&gt; identifies repeated attempts to perform the same operation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Database constraints&lt;/strong&gt; protect against duplicate inserts, including concurrent requests.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Transactions and carefully designed side effects&lt;/strong&gt; help maintain consistency across the entire workflow.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;No single layer solves every problem.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The goal isn't just to make an API request succeed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It's to make sure retries, failures, and concurrent requests don't produce unintended results.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Final thoughts
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Building reliable software requires thinking beyond the happy path.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A submit button can be disabled. A network request can fail. A server can process the same operation more than once.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The application should still behave predictably.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A combination of frontend safeguards, idempotency, database constraints, and transaction handling is generally more reliable than depending on any one of them.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What's your approach to preventing duplicate requests in production applications?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Further reading:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/sql-insert.html" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;PostgreSQL INSERT / ON CONFLICT documentation&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We've never had so much power to build software. You can turn an idea into a working app in a day/hour/minute? But there's always a catch: You AI agent can solve it too. So the question is: &lt;strong&gt;does anyone actually need what we/you/me're building?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I've been working in product development, open source, and startups for years, and this question has never felt more closer to home.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Every day we see more and more apps, but it's not always clear who the end user is or what problem it's going solving.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We've been working on our data grid library, &lt;a href="https://rv-grid.com/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;RevoGrid&lt;/a&gt;, for quite a few years now (closer to decade if we count for Open Source part). It's an Excel-like data grid you can put into your app.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Over this time, we've built so many components and tried so many approaches that you could probably build an entire Power BI on top of it. We've spent even more time testing it and solving quests of "what to do next?"&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So recently, we spent a week of our lives extracting these components into a &lt;a href="https://ui.rv-grid.com/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;separate UI library&lt;/a&gt; and making it available to everyone.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What's inside?&lt;/strong&gt; Pretty much the building blocks we've needed for our own products: charts, AI chat interfaces, file viewers, date pickers, dropdowns, forms, time grids, timeline controls, and plenty of smaller UI components.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Basically, the stuff you need when building analytical dashboards, reporting tools, or project management apps. And it works with React, Vue, Angular, Svelte, and plain TypeScript.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We use it a lot ourselves. Pretty much all our interfaces are built with these components.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Not sure if the world needs another UI library, but here it is. It's simple, it's been tested in our own products, and maybe it'll find a place in yours too, especially if you don't want to do all the heavy lifting yourself.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Happy coding! (Or is it vibe coding now?)&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In January I posted a crypto bubble chart I'd built from scratch on Threads. Few people saw it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Ten months, one redesign and a lot of tests later, it's a library: &lt;strong&gt;OrbFlow&lt;/strong&gt;. It's a bubble chart for the web where the bubbles act like bubbles. They rise in when the chart scrolls into view, float, push each other apart, and pop when you tap one.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Today the beta is on npm, and I'd like you to try to break it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Try it in two minutes
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;npm i orbflow@beta&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;React&lt;/strong&gt; (18 or 19, works with Next.js):&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;import { OrbChart } from "orbflow/react";

export default function App() {
  return (
    &amp;lt;div style={{ width: 600, height: 400 }}&amp;gt;
      &amp;lt;OrbChart
        data={[
          { id: "a", label: "Apples", value: 40 },
          { id: "b", label: "Pears", value: 25 },
          { id: "c", label: "Plums", value: 10 },
        ]}
        onOrbClick={(orb) =&amp;gt; console.log(orb.label)}
      /&amp;gt;
    &amp;lt;/div&amp;gt;
  );
}
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Plain JavaScript:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;import { OrbChart } from "orbflow";

const chart = new OrbChart("#chart");
chart.setData([
  { id: "a", label: "Apples", value: 40 },
  { id: "b", label: "Pears", value: 25 },
]);
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Give the container a size and the chart fills it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What's in the box
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Zero runtime dependencies, about 15 kB gzipped&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Bubble area follows the value, so twice the value really looks twice as big&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Tap to pop, with droplets, and popped bubbles can come back&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Keyboard and screen reader support: every bubble is a real button in a hidden list&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Respects "reduce motion": the chart settles and stays still&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;TypeScript types, ESM, CommonJS, and a CDN script&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Why a beta
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Once it's called 1.0, the API is a promise, and renaming anything after that breaks your app. So before I make that promise, I want to hear what feels wrong. The things I most want feedback on:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Were the prop and option names what you expected?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Did anything about sizing or layout surprise you?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;What data did you try, and did it read well as bubbles?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What's left before v1
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;[ ] Docs and API reference pass&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;[ ] Runnable examples&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;[ ] A speed pass for big charts&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;[ ] Your feedback&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Open an issue on &lt;a href="https://github.com/cascadingGeek/OrbFlow/issues" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;GitHub&lt;/a&gt; or comment here. Everyone whose feedback changes something gets credited in the v1 release notes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I'm building the rest in public on &lt;a href="https://www.threads.com/@cascadinggeek" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Threads&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://x.com/_cascadinggeek" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;X&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;


  How Does BreathTime’s Petition System Actually Work?
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;BreathTime is a small civic platform: people propose petitions, sign them, and leave a few words of support. This post walks through exactly how each of those steps works today — including the parts that are deliberately simple, and the parts that don't exist yet.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


  Step 1: Proposing a Petition
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Any registered, logged-in user can suggest a petition through the &lt;strong&gt;Propose a Petition&lt;/strong&gt; page. A proposal has three fields:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;a &lt;strong&gt;title&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;a &lt;strong&gt;description&lt;/strong&gt; of what should change and why&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;an &lt;strong&gt;addressee&lt;/strong&gt; — the government, institution or organisation the petition is aimed at&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The form is a React page that sends the proposal to an API route; the server checks that the user is signed in (NextAuth) and saves it to the database (Prisma + MySQL) with the status &lt;em&gt;pending&lt;/em&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


  Step 2: Moderation
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Every proposal is read by a person. In the admin panel, proposals are listed newest first, with the author's name. For each one, the administrator can:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;approve&lt;/strong&gt; it, or&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;reject&lt;/strong&gt; it,&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;optionally adding a comment. Either way, the author gets an in-app notification — and on a rejection, the admin's comment is included so they know why.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Approval is a decision, not an automatic switch: a live petition is then published by an administrator, who sets its title, description, addressee, category and signature goal (1,000 by default). Users can't publish petitions directly — that's on purpose for a platform this young. Every petition that goes live has been looked at by a human first.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


  Step 3: Signing
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Each petition has its own page (&lt;code&gt;/petitions/&amp;lt;id&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt;), rendered on the server with Next.js's App Router. When someone presses &lt;strong&gt;Sign&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;You must be logged in.&lt;/strong&gt; Guests can't sign; the request is refused until you sign in or register. The petition also has to be active.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;One person, one signature.&lt;/strong&gt; The server checks whether you've already signed, and the database enforces it too, with a unique constraint on &lt;em&gt;petition + user&lt;/em&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;The signature is saved.&lt;/strong&gt; It's stored together with an update to the petition's signature count, and the petition's author receives a notification.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Your page refreshes.&lt;/strong&gt; You see the new count straight away. Other visitors see it the next time they load the page — there are no live, real-time updates.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


  Step 4: Leaving a Comment
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When you sign, you can add an optional comment — a sentence on why it matters to you — and choose whether your signature is public. Public signatures, with their comments, are shown under the petition (the most recent 20).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There is no separate discussion thread yet, and no automatic word filter: comments are attached to signatures, and only people who have signed can leave one.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


  What Isn't There (Yet)
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An honest list, because it's an early-stage platform:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;No pagination&lt;/strong&gt; in the admin proposal list — fine at today's volume, not forever.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;No audit log&lt;/strong&gt; of admin decisions.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;No real-time updates&lt;/strong&gt; — counts and comments refresh on page load.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;No automated moderation&lt;/strong&gt; — every proposal is reviewed by a person.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


  The Stack Behind It
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Next.js 16 (App Router)&lt;/strong&gt; — server-rendered petition pages and API routes&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Prisma 5 + MySQL 8&lt;/strong&gt; — separate tables for petitions, signatures and proposals&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;NextAuth v4&lt;/strong&gt; (credentials + JWT) — sign-in and sessions&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Tailwind CSS v4&lt;/strong&gt; — the interface, available in 48 languages&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Three.js&lt;/strong&gt; — the animated globe on the homepage: yellow dots for the locations of registered users, green for users active in the last 15 minutes, and your own dot green when you're logged in, red when you're not&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


  A Real Example
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The first petition on the platform is the one BreathTime is named after: &lt;strong&gt;“BreathTime – globalny dzień oddechu dla naszej planety”&lt;/strong&gt; (&lt;em&gt;a global day of breath for our planet&lt;/em&gt;), addressed to the Polish government. It proposes that once a month, on the second Sunday, we pause commercial flights, cut combustion-vehicle traffic and scale back non-critical industrial production — as a constructive alternative to disruptive protest.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;At the time of writing it has &lt;strong&gt;8 signatures&lt;/strong&gt; towards a goal of 1,000. Small numbers, real people — which is exactly where every petition starts.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


  Wrapping Up
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Propose, review, sign, comment: four steps, each kept deliberately simple. If you have a cause worth putting in front of decision-makers, &lt;a href="https://breathtime.info/proposals" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;propose a petition&lt;/a&gt; — and if you think the process itself could work better, tell us.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;


  Server-Side Rendering on Country Subdomains: What We Got Wrong First
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;24ad.info is a classifieds site where every country has its own subdomain — &lt;code&gt;pl.24ad.info&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;de.24ad.info&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;gb.24ad.info&lt;/code&gt; — all served by one app. The frontend is a React 19 single-page app built with Vite, running on Express behind Caddy, with everything behind Cloudflare.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A single-page app has a well-known weakness: the first HTML a visitor (or a search engine) receives is mostly empty. Getting real content and correct metadata into that first response, &lt;em&gt;per country&lt;/em&gt;, took a few rounds. Here they are, including the one we rolled back the same day.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Round 1 (April): canonical URLs and the geo-redirect
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The first problems were about which URL counts as "the" page.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Canonical URLs always pointed at the main domain.&lt;/strong&gt; A category page on &lt;code&gt;pl.24ad.info&lt;/code&gt; told search engines its canonical address was the home page of &lt;code&gt;24ad.info&lt;/code&gt;. On 21 April that changed: the canonical now uses the host the request actually came in on, so &lt;code&gt;pl.24ad.info/c/…&lt;/code&gt; points to itself.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;The geo-redirect.&lt;/strong&gt; Visitors who arrive at the bare &lt;code&gt;24ad.info&lt;/code&gt; are sent to their country's subdomain, using Cloudflare's country header; countries we don't support go to the UK site (&lt;code&gt;gb.24ad.info&lt;/code&gt;). The redirect became a permanent &lt;code&gt;301&lt;/code&gt; instead of a temporary &lt;code&gt;302&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Who should &lt;em&gt;not&lt;/em&gt; be redirected.&lt;/strong&gt; Search-engine bots, logged-in users (an admin choosing the global view kept being bounced to their own country), and anyone who explicitly picks &lt;em&gt;Global&lt;/em&gt; — that choice adds &lt;code&gt;?global=1&lt;/code&gt;, which the redirect respects.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Round 2 (May): injecting data — and rolling half of it back
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On 19 May, version 1.4.3 started &lt;strong&gt;injecting data into the first response&lt;/strong&gt;. For the home page and for individual listings, the server fetched the data up front and embedded it in the HTML; the React app picked it up as its initial state. The result: no loading spinner, and the right &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;title&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; and meta tags from the very first byte.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For listing pages it worked. For the home page it caused a real bug: home shows the newest listings, and &lt;strong&gt;listings are country-specific&lt;/strong&gt; — but the injected data wasn't. Visitors could get a mix of UK and global listings on their own country's home page. The home-page injection was removed the same day.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The lesson was simple: anything you pre-render on a multi-country site has to be scoped by country exactly as the live page would be, or it's worse than nothing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Where it stands now
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Today the server renders three kinds of page with their own metadata and embedded initial data:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;the &lt;strong&gt;home page&lt;/strong&gt;,&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;listing pages&lt;/strong&gt; (&lt;code&gt;/posts/:slug&lt;/code&gt;),&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;category pages&lt;/strong&gt; (&lt;code&gt;/c/:slug&lt;/code&gt;),&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;with listings &lt;strong&gt;filtered by the country subdomain&lt;/strong&gt; the request came in on. Every other route gets the app's HTML template with its own canonical URL and meta tags. Unknown paths return a real &lt;code&gt;404&lt;/code&gt; marked &lt;code&gt;noindex&lt;/code&gt;, instead of quietly showing the home page.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There are no placeholders in the template: the server replaces the existing title, description, Open Graph and canonical tags, and adds a block of initial data. The template itself is never cached; server-rendered pages are cached at the Cloudflare edge for 60 seconds (with a further 5 minutes of serving stale while refreshing), and that cache is purged on every deploy. Static assets under &lt;code&gt;/assets&lt;/code&gt; are served directly by Caddy with a one-year immutable cache.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One Caddy site block covers both &lt;code&gt;24ad.info&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;*.24ad.info&lt;/code&gt;, using a wildcard certificate.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What we'd tell anyone doing the same
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Canonical URLs are per host&lt;/strong&gt;, not per site, once you use subdomains.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Decide who the geo-redirect must leave alone&lt;/strong&gt; — bots, logged-in users and explicit choices — before you ship it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Pre-rendered data must be scoped exactly like the live page.&lt;/strong&gt; If the live page filters by country, the server must too — or don't pre-render it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://dotsimplify.com" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;ReactJS&lt;/a&gt; continues to evolve as developers build increasingly complex web applications. Learning the fundamentals is important, but professional developers also need to understand modern development tools, architectural approaches, and emerging best practices.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Future-proofing ReactJS skills does not mean learning every new library immediately. It means understanding the underlying concepts and knowing how to select appropriate technologies for &lt;br&gt;
&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Feo5vrd1uiv0nzdtgmdmi.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Feo5vrd1uiv0nzdtgmdmi.png" alt=" " width="800" height="450"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;different projects.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Understand Modern JavaScript&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Strong JavaScript knowledge is the foundation of advanced React development.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Developers should understand concepts such as destructuring, spread operators, modules, promises, async and await, closures, and array methods.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example, methods such as map(), filter(), and reduce() are frequently used when working with application data.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Understanding these concepts helps developers write cleaner React components and solve complex programming problems.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Learn TypeScript with React&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;TypeScript adds static typing to JavaScript.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It helps developers identify certain errors during development rather than waiting until the application runs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;React developers can use TypeScript to define component props, state structures, event types, and API response formats.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;type UserProps = {&lt;br&gt;
  name: string;&lt;br&gt;
  age: number;&lt;br&gt;
};&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;function UserCard({ name, age }: UserProps) {&lt;br&gt;
  return (&lt;br&gt;
    &lt;/p&gt;
&lt;br&gt;
      &lt;h2&gt;{name}&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;br&gt;
      &lt;p&gt;{age}&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;br&gt;
    &lt;br&gt;
  );&lt;br&gt;
}

&lt;p&gt;This makes component expectations clearer and improves development experience in larger projects.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Explore Modern React Features&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Developers should stay familiar with modern React capabilities.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Features such as transitions, Suspense, Server Components, and improved rendering APIs support different application requirements.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;React Server Components allow compatible frameworks and environments to render certain components on the server without shipping their component implementation to the browser.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Developers should understand the distinction between server and client components and the restrictions associated with each.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Learn a Modern React Framework&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;React is a library for building user interfaces, and developers often use additional tools to build complete applications.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Frameworks such as Next.js provide features including routing, server rendering, static generation, and server-side functionality.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Understanding these capabilities helps developers build applications that go beyond isolated frontend components.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;However, the choice of framework should depend on project requirements rather than popularity alone.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Improve Development with Vite&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Vite is a modern frontend build tool that provides fast development startup and efficient development workflows.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It supports React projects and offers features such as hot module replacement.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Developers can use it to create projects, organize source files, and configure development environments.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Understanding build tools is important because modern React applications rely on bundling, transformation, and optimization processes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Follow Accessibility Standards&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Accessibility should be an essential part of every development project.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Developers should use semantic HTML elements, appropriate labels, keyboard navigation, and sufficient colour contrast.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;ARIA attributes should be used when necessary to communicate additional information to assistive technologies.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Accessible applications are easier to use and provide a better experience for a wider audience.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Understand Server-Side Rendering&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Server-Side Rendering (SSR) allows web pages to be rendered on the server before being delivered to the browser.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It can improve initial content availability and help search engines discover page content, depending on the application's implementation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Developers should also understand hydration, which connects the server-rendered HTML to the client-side React application.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Incorrect server and client rendering assumptions can lead to hydration errors.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Build Real-World Projects&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Reading documentation is important, but practical experience is equally valuable.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Developers should build projects that involve different React concepts.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Useful examples include:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An e-commerce product catalogue.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A task management application.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A weather dashboard.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A personal portfolio.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An employee management system.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A blog with search and filtering.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;These projects help developers understand how components, state, routing, APIs, and testing work together.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Learn Git and Collaborative Development&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Professional development often involves working with other developers.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Git helps teams track code changes, manage branches, review modifications, and collaborate on projects.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Developers should understand commits, branches, merges, pull requests, and conflict resolution.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;These skills are important for working effectively in professional software development environments.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Keep Learning Through Documentation&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Technology changes quickly, and developers should develop the habit of reading official documentation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The official React documentation is an excellent resource for understanding modern React concepts and recommended practices.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Experimenting with small examples and reviewing existing projects can also improve technical understanding.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Writing functional code is only one part of successful application development. Developers must also ensure that their applications behave correctly under different conditions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Testing plays an important role in identifying errors, preventing unexpected behaviour, and maintaining application quality.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://dotsimplify.com" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;ReactJS&lt;/a&gt; developers can use several testing techniques to verify individual components, user interactions, and complete application workflows.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Understand the Importance of Component Testing&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;React applications are built using reusable components.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A component may display information, accept user input, or perform specific actions when users interact with it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Component testing helps developers verify whether individual components behave as expected.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example, a login button should respond correctly when clicked, while an input field should accept and display the entered value.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Testing components independently makes it easier to identify problems before they affect the entire application.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Use Jest for JavaScript Testing&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Jest is a widely used JavaScript testing framework.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It provides features for writing test cases, checking expected results, mocking functions, and measuring code coverage.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Developers can use Jest to test utility functions, data-processing logic, and other JavaScript operations used within React applications.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;function add(a, b) {&lt;br&gt;
  return a + b;&lt;br&gt;
}&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;test("adds two numbers", () =&amp;gt; {&lt;br&gt;
  expect(add(2, 3)).toBe(5);&lt;br&gt;
});&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This simple test checks whether the function produces the expected output.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Explore React Testing Library&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;React Testing Library helps developers test components from the user's perspective.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Rather than focusing on internal implementation details, it encourages testing what users can actually see and interact with.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example, developers can verify whether a heading appears, a button responds to a click, or an error message is displayed after an invalid form submission.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This approach creates tests that are more closely connected to actual application behaviour.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Test User Interactions&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Testing user interactions is important because many application features depend on user input.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Common examples include:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Clicking buttons.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Submitting forms.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Selecting dropdown options.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Opening modal windows.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Navigating between pages.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Filtering and sorting information.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The user-event utility from Testing Library can simulate realistic user interactions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Developers should verify both the action and its expected result.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Mock API Requests&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Components that communicate with external APIs should be tested under different response conditions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Mocking allows developers to simulate successful requests, server errors, and delayed responses without depending on an actual backend service.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Tools such as Mock Service Worker (MSW) can intercept network requests and provide controlled responses.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This makes tests more predictable and easier to maintain.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Implement End-to-End Testing&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Unit and component tests are valuable, but they do not always reveal problems involving multiple parts of an application.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;End-to-end testing checks complete user workflows.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example, an e-commerce test may involve opening a product page, adding an item to the cart, entering checkout details, and completing an order through a test environment.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Tools such as Playwright and Cypress are useful for automating these workflows.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Test Error and Loading Conditions&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Developers should not test only successful scenarios.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A reliable application must also handle failed requests, missing information, invalid inputs, and unavailable services.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Tests should verify whether the application displays appropriate loading indicators and meaningful error messages.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This helps prevent unexpected behaviour in real-world environments.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Measure Code Coverage Carefully&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Code coverage indicates how much of the application's code is executed during testing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It can help identify untested sections, but high coverage does not automatically mean that an application is free from bugs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Developers should focus on meaningful test cases rather than writing unnecessary tests simply to achieve a particular percentage.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Include Testing in the Development Workflow&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Testing should be integrated into the regular development process.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Automated tests can run whenever developers submit code changes through a continuous integration pipeline.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This helps identify problems early and reduces the risk of introducing regressions into production applications.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Forms are an essential feature of modern web applications. Users interact with forms to register accounts, submit feedback, place orders, update personal information, and complete online transactions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Although &lt;a href="https://dotsimplify.com" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;ReactJS&lt;/a&gt; provides basic tools for handling forms, complex forms require a more organized approach.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Advanced form-handling techniques help developers reduce unnecessary code, improve validation, and create a better user experience.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Understand Controlled Components&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A controlled component is an input element whose value is managed through React state.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;function LoginForm() {&lt;br&gt;
  const [email, setEmail] = useState("");&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;return (&lt;br&gt;

      type="email"&lt;br&gt;
      value={email}&lt;br&gt;
      onChange={event =&amp;gt; setEmail(event.target.value)}&lt;br&gt;
    /&amp;gt;&lt;br&gt;
  );&lt;br&gt;
}&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;React controls the input value, making it easier to validate information and respond to user interactions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Controlled components are particularly useful for forms that require conditional fields or real-time feedback.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Understand Uncontrolled Components&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Uncontrolled components store their current values in the DOM rather than React state.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Developers can access these values through refs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This approach may be useful for simple forms or when integrating with certain third-party libraries.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Choosing between controlled and uncontrolled components depends on the complexity of the form and the application's requirements.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Use Formik or React Hook Form&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Writing validation and submission logic manually for every form can create repetitive code.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Libraries such as React Hook Form and Formik simplify form management.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;React Hook Form is particularly useful for reducing unnecessary re-renders and managing complex form inputs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Developers can use these libraries to handle registration forms, checkout pages, surveys, and multi-step applications.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Implement Schema-Based Validation&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Schema-based validation allows developers to define rules separately from the user interface.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Libraries such as Zod and Yup can define conditions for email addresses, passwords, phone numbers, and other fields.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example, a registration form might require a valid email address and a password containing a minimum number of characters.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Centralized validation makes it easier to maintain consistent rules across multiple forms.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Provide Real-Time Feedback&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Users should understand why an input is invalid and how to correct it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example, an email field can display a clear error message when the entered value does not match the expected format.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;However, developers should avoid overwhelming users with too many messages while they are still typing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Validation timing should depend on the field and the overall form experience.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Create Multi-Step Forms&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Some applications require lengthy forms containing multiple sections.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Instead of displaying every field on one screen, developers can divide the form into steps.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example, an online application may collect personal details, educational information, and supporting documents separately.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The application should preserve entered information when users move between steps.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It should also provide progress indicators and allow users to review their answers before final submission.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Handle Asynchronous Validation&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Certain validation rules require communication with a server.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example, an application may need to check whether a username is already registered.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Developers should handle loading indicators, server errors, and delayed responses carefully.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Debouncing can reduce unnecessary API requests when users type into fields that require remote validation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Improve Accessibility&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Every input should have an associated label.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Error messages should be descriptive, and important validation information should be available to screen readers.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Developers should also ensure that users can navigate forms using a keyboard.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Accessible forms are easier to use for everyone, not just users relying on assistive technologies.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Secure Form Submission&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Frontend validation improves usability but cannot replace backend validation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;All submitted information should be checked again on the server.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Sensitive information should be transmitted through HTTPS, and applications should implement suitable protections against malicious submissions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Conclusion&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Navigation is an essential part of every modern web application. Users expect to move between pages, access specific sections, and return to previously visited content without unnecessary page reloads.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://dotsimplify.com" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;React Router&lt;/a&gt; is a widely used routing library that helps developers manage navigation in React applications.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Although basic routing is relatively easy to implement, advanced routing concepts provide greater flexibility when developing large applications.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Understand Client-Side Routing&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Traditional websites often request a completely new HTML page whenever a user clicks a navigation link.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;React applications commonly use client-side routing, where the browser URL changes while the application updates the displayed content.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This provides a smoother navigation experience and can reduce unnecessary full-page reloads.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;React Router helps developers connect URL paths with the appropriate components.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Organize Routes Effectively&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A well-organized routing structure makes applications easier to maintain.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example, an e-commerce application may contain routes for the home page, product listings, individual products, shopping cart, and checkout.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Developers should group related routes logically and use consistent naming conventions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For larger applications, route configuration can be separated into dedicated files.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Use Dynamic Route Parameters&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Dynamic routes allow developers to display different content using a shared component.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;} /&amp;gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The product component can retrieve the productId from the URL and use it to request the corresponding product information.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This approach eliminates the need to create separate routes for every product.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Implement Nested Routes&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Nested routing is useful when multiple pages share a common layout.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Consider an administrative dashboard with sections for users, reports, analytics, and settings.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Instead of repeating the dashboard navigation and sidebar in every page, developers can create a parent route with an outlet for displaying nested content.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This improves code reuse and creates a consistent user experience.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Protect Private Routes&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Some application sections should only be accessible to authenticated users.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example, a banking dashboard or personal account page should require authentication.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Developers can implement route-level authentication checks to redirect unauthenticated users to a login page.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;However, frontend route protection is only a user-interface convenience. The backend must still verify authentication and authorization for every protected operation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Implement Lazy Loading&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Large applications may contain many pages that users do not need immediately.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;React Router can work with React's lazy loading capabilities to load certain route components only when they are required.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This reduces the initial JavaScript bundle and can improve loading performance.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A suitable fallback interface should be displayed while the component is loading.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Handle Navigation Errors&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Users may enter incorrect URLs, follow outdated links, or attempt to access unavailable pages.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A professional application should include a meaningful not-found page.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Developers should also provide suitable error handling for failed data requests and unavailable resources.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;React Router supports route error handling features that can help organize these experiences.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Use URL Search Parameters&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Search parameters are useful when a page needs to preserve filters, sorting preferences, or pagination information.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example, a product listing page may use parameters such as category, price range, and page number.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Keeping this information in the URL allows users to share specific views and revisit them through browser history.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Improve Accessibility&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Navigation should be accessible to keyboard users and people using assistive technologies.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Developers should use semantic links, descriptive labels, appropriate focus management, and clear page headings.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;After important navigation changes, the application should communicate the new page context to users.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Security is a critical part of modern&lt;a href="https://dotsimplify.com" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt; web development&lt;/a&gt;. ReactJS provides powerful tools for creating interactive interfaces, but developers must follow appropriate security practices to protect application data and prevent vulnerabilities.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A secure React application requires attention to user input, authentication, authorization, API communication, and dependency management.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Understanding these areas can help developers create reliable applications that protect users and their information.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Validate User Input&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;User input should never be considered automatically trustworthy.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Applications frequently accept information through registration forms, contact forms, search boxes, and comment sections.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Developers should validate data on both the client and server sides.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Client-side validation improves the user experience by identifying incorrect information before submission. Server-side validation is essential because attackers can bypass browser-based checks.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Libraries such as Zod and Yup can help developers define reusable validation schemas.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Understand Cross-Site Scripting&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) occurs when malicious scripts are injected into web pages and executed in another user's browser.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;React automatically escapes text values rendered through JSX, which provides protection against many common injection attacks.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;However, developers must be careful when using dangerouslySetInnerHTML.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If HTML must be displayed from an external source, it should be sanitized using an appropriate and maintained sanitization library.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Never assume that external HTML is safe simply because it comes from a trusted-looking source.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Implement Secure Authentication&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Authentication verifies a user's identity before allowing access to an application.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;React developers often integrate authentication through a backend service or an identity provider.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A secure authentication system should use appropriate password hashing on the server, secure session management, and protected communication through HTTPS.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For browser-based applications, secure, HttpOnly, and appropriately configured SameSite cookies can help protect session credentials.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Sensitive authentication tokens should not be casually stored in localStorage because scripts running in the page can access them.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Apply Authorization Checks&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Authentication and authorization are different concepts.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Authentication determines who the user is, while authorization determines what the user can access.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example, an employee may be allowed to view a dashboard but not modify administrative settings.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;React can hide or display interface elements according to user permissions. However, frontend checks alone are not security controls.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The backend must independently verify permissions before processing protected requests.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Protect API Communication&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;React applications frequently communicate with external APIs to retrieve and submit information.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Developers should use HTTPS and avoid exposing private API keys or sensitive credentials in frontend code.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Anything included in a client-side JavaScript bundle should be treated as publicly accessible.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Private operations should be performed through a secure backend.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Developers should also implement suitable error handling, request validation, and rate limiting on the server side.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Protect Against CSRF&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF) occurs when an attacker tricks a browser into sending an unwanted authenticated request.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Applications using cookie-based authentication should implement appropriate CSRF protections.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;These may include SameSite cookie settings, anti-CSRF tokens, and server-side origin checks, depending on the architecture.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Developers should understand the authentication mechanism before choosing the correct protection.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Keep Dependencies Updated&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;React applications often depend on external packages for routing, forms, styling, and state management.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Outdated dependencies can contain known security vulnerabilities.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Developers should regularly review installed packages, remove unnecessary dependencies, and apply security updates.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Tools such as npm audit can help identify known vulnerabilities in the dependency tree.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Handle Errors Without Revealing Sensitive Information&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Error messages should provide useful information without exposing passwords, authentication tokens, internal server paths, or confidential system details.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;React Error Boundaries can help prevent certain rendering errors from crashing the entire user interface.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;However, they do not replace backend error handling or comprehensive monitoring.&lt;/p&gt;
